steps.body:
Effects are the hook people reach for most often by mistake. Every effect is more work after a render, and often a second render too. Code spread across effects is hard to follow, and it causes a common bug: for a moment, the page shows values that don't match each other. So before you write one, ask: is this code keeping something outside React in step, because the component is on the screen? If not, there's a better place for it.

## Derive values during render

When you can compute something from props or state, compute it while rendering. Don't copy it into more state and update that state in an effect. The first example does it the wrong way: it keeps the filtered list in state and refreshes it in an effect. Its log shows the cost. When you type, React renders with the new search but the old list, commits that to the page, runs the effect, and only then renders again with the right list. For one frame, the results don't match the search box.

The fix is shorter than the bug:

```tsx
const q = query.toLowerCase();
const visible = products.filter((p) => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(q));
```

One render, and the list always matches the query. Filtering a few hundred products takes well under a millisecond, so doing it on every render is fine. For calculations that really are slow, the React Compiler in your project keeps the last result and reuses it while its inputs stay the same, and the Re-rendering and Performance lesson shows how. The same goes for a cart's total from its items, a full name from two fields, or whether a form is ready to send: keep state minimal, as the State and Events lesson said, and work out the rest as you render. The third example shows the linter catching the effect version.

## Put what the user did in the event handler

When code runs because the user did something, put it in the handler for that action, not in an effect that watches for the state the action leaves behind. Here's a checkout that places the order in an effect once a flag turns on:

```tsx
const [submitted, setSubmitted] = useState(false);

useEffect(() => {
  if (submitted) {
    placeOrder(cart);
    showToast("Order placed");
  }
}, [submitted, cart]);
```

It has two bugs. The effect runs again whenever `cart` changes while `submitted` is still true, so a second order goes out. And in development StrictMode runs it twice on mount, which is a warning that anything that mounts it again would order again too. The handler knows exactly what happened and when:

```tsx
function handlePlaceOrder() {
  placeOrder(cart);
  showToast("Order placed");
}
```

A rule to remember: code that runs because the component **appeared** goes in an effect, and code that runs because the user **did something** goes in a handler.

## Reset state with a key

A product page shows a review form that keeps the draft in state. Go from the tent to the stove, and the tent's half-written review is still in the box. React kept the same component, because it's the same type in the same place in the tree, and the Re-rendering and Performance lesson explains why that keeps its state. The tempting fix is an effect that clears the draft when the product changes, but that renders once with the wrong draft first. Use a `key` instead, the same prop that tells list items apart:

```tsx
<ReviewForm key={product.slug} slug={product.slug} />
```

When the key changes, React treats the form as a different component. It unmounts the old one, along with all its state, and mounts a fresh one.

## Fetching data

Loading data when a page appears does keep something outside React in step, the server, so it's a real use for an effect. But a fetch written by hand in an effect has traps: two responses can arrive in the wrong order, nothing is kept for when you come back to the page, and StrictMode sends every request twice in development. The Calling APIs From React lesson walks through each one, and the library most teams use instead.

